  {
   "id": "concept2",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Time constant and charging"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "When a step voltage V is applied to a series RC circuit, the capacitor voltage rises as $v_C = V(1 - e^{-t/RC})$ and the resistor voltage falls as $v_R = Ve^{-t/RC}$. The time constant $\\tau = RC$ is the time to reach 63.2% of V. After 5τ the capacitor is practically fully charged (99.3%). Whether an RC circuit differentiates or integrates depends on τ compared with the period of the input."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "vC = V(1 − e^(−t/RC))"
    },
    {
     "en": "τ = RC: 63.2% of V"
    },
    {
     "en": "5τ: practically full (99.3%)"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "The time constant RC is the time for the capacitor to reach sixty-three percent of the supply. After five time constants it is practically full.",
    "kn": "ಕಾಲ ಸ್ಥಿರಾಂಕ τ = RC ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಧಾರಕ ಸರಬರಾಜಿನ 63.2% ತಲುಪುತ್ತದೆ. 5τ ನಂತರ ಬಹುತೇಕ ಪೂರ್ಣ ಆವೇಶಗೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ."
   },
   "formula": "v_C = V\\left(1 - e^{-t/RC}\\right), \\qquad \\tau = RC"
  },
